          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Do I need a parking pass for hiking trails near Manchester or Concord?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Most New Hampshire state park trailheads charge a day-use parking fee, including popular Lakes Region
              spots. The White Mountain National Forest requires its own parking pass at most trailheads — a day, week
              or annual pass sold at ranger stations and many trailhead kiosks. Free parking is more common at town
              conservation land close to Manchester and Concord themselves.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Can I hike near Manchester or Concord in winter?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, but conditions vary sharply with elevation. The close-in Lakes Region cluster around Mount Kearsarge
              and Mount Major stays walkable with basic traction most winters. The White Mountains are a different
              matter — Mount Washington's summit sees some of the most extreme winter weather in the country, and
              full winter mountaineering gear and experience are essential above treeline from December through April.
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
